So the last put decrements refs to 0 AND drops the last GEM reference in the same call — p7580_gem_free_object() runs at that instant and frees the BO/leaves, i.e. exactly when the old code called p7580_gem_gpu_unmap(). The page still dies at the last node's retire; nothing extends its lifetime. The new comment in the function says the mapping is torn down by gem_free_object, but gem_free_object is reached at the same moment, so the GPU still follows a stale chain.

What actually pins the page for the file's life (cheapest correct form):
 a) In shadow_create, take ONE extra drm_gem_object_reference(obj) and hang the GEM object (not sh — sh is kfree'd at last put) on a small per-file list, e.g. drv_file->shadow_pins. Do not touch sh->refs; the node accounting must stay nodes.
 b) In the file's postclose/release path, after the slots are quiescent (drm_sched_fini / entity quiesce already there), walk the list and drm_gem_object_unreference_unlocked() each, then free the list nodes. That keeps leaves alive while any GPU work that can still read them exists.
 (b') If you prefer the pure test variant instead of the pool: skip both the final unreference in shadow_put and the iova free, and skip the unmap. Wrong for production, but it isolates "is the page's lifetime the whole bug" with no accounting subtleties.

